  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe Upper Ohio River Valley remains on the western periphery of\na fairly stagnant closed low over the New England region; such\npattern does offer low probability afternoon shower and \nthunderstorm chances today mainly over the higher terrain SE of\nPittsburgh (where convergence best aligns). It otherwise fosters\ndry weather with seasonable temperature that sees subtle\ntemperature rises to end the week as pressure heights aloft\nslowly increase.\n\nThe weekend outlook features the next trough system slowly \ncrossing the region while generally sitting well south of the\nmain jet stream farther north. Modest moisture returns\ncombined with trough-induced lift likely results in an uptick in\nwidespread showers and thunderstorms favoring the afternoon to\nearly evening hours. Pending the trough depth and eastward\ntrack, there are low probability scenarios to amplify moisture\nreturns in SW flow such that it creates a low-end flash flood\nrisk (PWATs rise toward or above the 90th percentile; weak-flow\naids slow-moving storms; enough CAPE to allow for warm-rain \nprocesses). This bears monitoring in what otherwise appears to\nbe a low impact pattern given meager CAPE and shear values.",
